.TH EUCLID-CLI-EMO-CREATE-ALERT-RULE 1 "October 2026" "euclid-cli 2.1.3" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-emo-create-alert-rule \- create a threshold alert rule on a metric
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli emo create-alert-rule
.B \-\-rule\-id
.I ID
.B \-\-metric
.I NAME
[\fB\-\-label\fR \fINAME\fR=\fIVALUE\fR]...
[\fB\-\-statistic\fR \fBvalue\fR|\fBmax\fR|\fBmin\fR]
[\fB\-\-comparison\fR \fBabove\fR|\fBbelow\fR]
[\fB\-\-warning\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-critical\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-for\-periods\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-clear\-periods\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-data\-after\-periods\fR \fIN\fR]
[\fB\-\-description\fR \fITEXT\fR]
[\fB\-\-disabled\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Creates a rule that judges one metric every period \- five minutes as
shipped \- and raises an alert when a series crosses a line. See
.BR euclid-cli-emo (1)
for the states a series moves through and the events each change
publishes.
.PP
Without
.BR \-\-label ,
the rule judges every series of the metric, each on its own: a rule on
.I system-cpu-usage
alerts per host, and picks up a host that starts reporting later
without being told. With
.BR \-\-label ,
only the series carrying every label given, with that value; a series
may carry more labels than the rule names.
.PP
The first evaluation is at the next period's flush;
.BR euclid-cli-emo-list-alerts (1)
shows a series only once the rule has judged a row of it.
.SH CHOOSING THE STATISTIC
A row holds a period's
.I value
\- its mean for a gauge, its sum for a rate \- and its
.I max
and
.IR min .
.B value
says how the period went as a whole, and a thirty-second stall in a
five-minute period barely moves it.
.B max
sees the stall, and with it every short burst: more alerts, sooner.
.B min
is for a rule alerting below \- the lowest free disk in the period, the
quietest moment of a heartbeat.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-rule\-id " " \fIID\fR
The rule's name, unique in the account. Letters, digits,
.BR \- ,
.B _
and
.BR . ,
at most 128. Required.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-metric " " \fINAME\fR
The metric to judge, as
.BR euclid-cli-emo-list-metrics (1)
names it. Required.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-label " " \fINAME\fR=\fIVALUE\fR
Only series with this label. Repeatable; every one must match.
.TP
.BR \-\-statistic " " \fBvalue\fR|\fBmax\fR|\fBmin\fR
Which figure of a row is judged. Default
.BR value .
.TP
.BR \-\-comparison " " \fBabove\fR|\fBbelow\fR
Which side of the lines is the wrong one. Default
.BR above .
Queue depth and latency are bad above; free disk and a heartbeat rate
below.
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-warning " " \fIN\fR
The warning line. Past it is strictly past it: a value equal to the line
is inside.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-critical " " \fIN\fR
The critical line. At least one of the two is required. With both, the
warning line must come first: below the critical one for
.BR above ,
above it for
.BR below .
.TP
.BR \-\-for\-periods " " \fIN\fR
Periods past a line, running, before WARNING or CRITICAL is entered.
Default 1. A period past the critical line counts as past the warning
line too.
.TP
.BR \-\-clear\-periods " " \fIN\fR
Periods back inside, running, before the state is left. Default 1;
2 or more keeps a value sitting on a line from raising and clearing an
alert every period.
.TP
.BR \-\-no\-data\-after\-periods " " \fIN\fR
Periods without a row before a series goes NO_DATA. Default 0, never.
Worth setting for anything whose silence is itself the problem \- a host
that stops reporting has usually stopped.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-description " " \fITEXT\fR
What the rule is for. Shown beside every alert it raises.
.TP
.B \-\-disabled
Store the rule without evaluating it yet; enable it with
.BR euclid-cli-emo-update-alert-rule (1).
.SH LIMITS
One state is kept per series a rule judges, so a rule with no label on a
metric labelled per instance or per queue is a state per instance or per
queue. A rule matching more series than
.I euclid.modules.emo.alert.max-series-per-rule
(200 as shipped) is refused \- narrow it with
.BR \-\-label .
Series appearing later beyond that limit are not judged, and EMO logs a
warning saying so.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The rule was created and is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
The caller was not authenticated or is not an administrator; a rule of
that id already exists (HTTP 409); or the rule is not valid, with the
reason printed (HTTP 400).
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
# CPU per host: warning above 80%, critical above 90%
euclid-cli emo create-alert-rule --rule-id system-cpu-per-host \\
    --metric system-cpu-usage --warning 80 --critical 90 --clear-periods 2

# One worker saturated for ten minutes
euclid-cli emo create-alert-rule --rule-id pi-load --metric system-load-per-core \\
    --label host=vogje01-pios --statistic max --warning 0.7 --critical 0.9 \\
    --for-periods 2 --no-data-after-periods 3

# Less than 50 GB left on the data volume
euclid-cli emo create-alert-rule --rule-id data-disk --metric disk-available \\
    --comparison below --statistic min --warning 100e9 --critical 50e9
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-emo (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emo-list-metrics (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emo-update-alert-rule (1),
.BR euclid-cli-emo-list-alerts (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1)
